{ Source: John Hummel, Python LISA 1.00, DATA/lovetri/lovetri9.sym (unchanged below this header).
{ Reference outcome: lovetri9.bat (2007, Vers142, learning rate 1.0): Amy->Abe, Bill->Beth, Cat->Chad in 10/10 runs.
{ 2003 Table 2: the structurally correct, cross-gender mapping needs both propositions in one phase set.
Note: LoveTri9 Made from lovetri4
Note: difficult mapping but no inference or groups (keep it simple)
Note: purpose is to test mapping algorithm
Analog Amy&Bill
  Defpreds
    Loves 2 emotion positive strong loves ;
    Jealous 2 emotion negative strong jealous ;
  end; { def preds
  Defobjs
     Amy human adult female amy ;
     Bill human adult male bill ;
     Cat  human adult female cat ;
  end; { def objs
  DefProps
     P1 Loves ( Amy Bill ) ; { NOTE: New prop syntax! 
     P2 Loves ( Bill Cat ) ; {       Pred on the OUTSIDE of the parens!
  end ; { def props
done ; { analog 1
Analog Abe&Beth
  Defpreds
    Loves 2 emotion positive strong loves ;
  end ; { def preds
  Defobjs
     Abe human adult male abe ;
     Beth human adult female beth ;
     Chad  human adult male chad ;
  end ; { def objs
  DefProps
     P1 Loves ( Abe Beth ) ;
     P2 Loves ( Beth Chad ) ;
  end ; { defprops
done ; { analog 2
Sequence
  Driver=[ 0 ]
  Recip=[ 1 ]
  Order=[ P1 P2 h P1 h P2 h ]
Done. { whole sym file
